# Hand-copied backups. `Manuscript_PLOSONE_v2.md.bak` is a 2026-10-03 ancestor
# of the manuscript -- 363 lines against the current 374, missing the retitled
# abstract -- so it is not a snapshot of any state anyone would want back. It is
# also the only `.bak` in the tree and nothing ignored it, which made it the one
# file here that `git add -A` would have committed *as* a backup. Backups by
# copy are what tracking the manuscript source replaces.
*.bak

# Peer-review material. NEVER publish this repository's peer-review
# correspondence: the editor's decision letter and the reviewers' comments are
# confidential, and the response letters quote them. This repository is public,
# and it is also the working directory, so submission packages sit in the same
# tree as the analysis code and a plain `git add -A` would sweep them in.
# `pre_publish_check.py` enforces the same rule by reading what git would
# actually stage, because a path list only covers names someone thought of.
# The submission-directory rules carry a trailing `*` on the directory name
# (`*_Submission*/*`, not `*_Submission/*`) on purpose. When the v2 package was
# assembled it went into a sibling directory called PLOS_ONE_Submission_v2, and
# the narrower pattern silently failed to match it: everything in that folder --
# the manuscript, the figures, and the assembly checklist that quotes the
# reviewers' points by number -- stayed stageable while the rule that was meant
# to exclude it sat right there looking correct. `*_Submission*` matches both
# PLOS_ONE_Submission and PLOS_ONE_Submission_v2, and any future suffix.
#
# That fix was still one level short. The rules below originally named the three
# extensions that mattered at the time (`*_Submission*/*.docx`, `*.pdf`, `*.md`),
# which covers a file sitting directly in a submission directory and nothing
# else. Everything nested one level deeper stayed stageable: BBA_Submission's
# figure PNGs and supplementary CSVs, Frontiers_Submission's figure TIFFs,
# HC_Submission/Figures/Main_Figures.pdf. `*_Submission*/**` is the rule those
# three were trying to be -- the whole directory, at any depth. The extension
# list was the bug: a rule written against the file types of one package silently
# fails for the next package, which is the same failure the trailing-`*` note
# above records one level up.
#
# HC_Submission is a deliberate exception in substance but not in mechanism: its
# manuscript, cover letter and figures were committed before this rule existed
# and remain tracked, because gitignore never untracks. Adding to it later needs
# an explicit `git add -f`, which is the right amount of friction for a
# directory that holds another journal's submission package.
*Decision_Letter*
*Reviewer_Comments*
*Response_to_Reviewers*
rebuttal*
# The line-editing script for the response letter is the letter in another
# form: it carries both sides of every replacement it applies, so the "old"
# strings are the letter's own sentences. Neither rule above catches it -- the
# name says "response_letter", not "Response_to_Reviewers", and the prose it
# embeds contains none of the gate's content markers. It sat untracked and
# unignored, which is the one state where `git add -A` publishes it, and only a
# manual read kept it out. That is not a defence that survives the next session.
*line_edit_response_letter*
# Cover letters belong to the same rule for a reason of substance rather than
# of file type. A revision cover letter is not a neutral submission note:
# Cover_Letter_PLOSONE_v2.md thanks the reviewers by count and concedes that
# their central objection is correct, which is peer-review correspondence
# however the file is named. The gate below does not catch it --
# `pre_publish_check.py` blocks on the filenames listed here, and a cover
# letter's name is not one of them, so it returned exit 0 with the file still
# stageable. The letter is also a build source, since `assemble_plos_v2.py`
# pandoc's it into 01_Cover_Letter.docx; but the response letter sits in
# exactly that position and is ignored anyway, so the precedent is already set
# that confidentiality outranks tracking a source.
Cover_Letter_*
*_Submission*/**
v2_output/logs/transcript_msg_*.txt

# Editable figure sources. R17 renders these SVG/PDF intermediates on its way to
# the Fig*.pdf the package ships, and nothing reads them back -- they are derived
# and regenerable by re-running R17. They were untracked and unignored, which is
# the one state that matters here: `git add -A` would have committed 20-odd
# intermediate files that no one chose to track, and the published PNG/PDF
# alongside them would have disagreed the first time a font moved.
v2_output/figures/editable/
